== 1. two routes that share NO tie and ALL their evidence.
      i is a member of g and of g'.  Both have the same six other members j1..j6; each j was seen once with v.
      (§3 creates a new category unit for every frame slot, so such twins are the normal case.) ==
   W(g -> v) = W(g' -> v) = 6   (the same six observations, copied twice by the Up rule)
   the conclusion i -> v is produced by the chain  i -> g -> v.
   'evidence that does not lie on the chain' = own evidence of i -> v (none) + what the Down rule brings
   through every other route, here i -> g' -> v:
     1 twin category besides g:  W_outside =  3.0  ->  R = 0.875
     2 twin categories besides g:  W_outside =  6.0  ->  R = 0.929
     5 twin categories besides g:  W_outside = 15.0  ->  R = 0.969
   by EVIDENCE the outside is empty (the six observations are the chain): R should be 'unknown', not 0.875-0.97.
   Leave-path-out removes ties; the Up rule has already copied their evidence onto other ties.

== 2. 'the chain that produced it' is not defined by §2.2 (settling sums ALL incoming ties in parallel).
      i is a member of g (six members seen with v) and of h (four members seen WITHOUT v). ==
   if the producing chain is i -> g -> v:  R = e from the h-route alone = 0.167
   if the producing chain is i -> h -> v:  R = e from the g-route alone = 0.875
   what settling actually computes (both routes at once):               e = 0.583
   Same state, same tie, three numbers.  R is a function of an attribution the dynamics do not make.

== 3. shared prefix.  Two chains  i -> a -> b -> v  and  i -> a -> c -> v  (EXPECT ties). ==
   Leaving out the first chain removes the tie i -> a.  The second chain starts with the same tie, so nothing
   reaches v any more:  W_outside = 0  ->  R = 0.500, although a -> c -> v is independent evidence for the
   second half.  With one category (or one context) per instance this is the general case: every route out of
   i shares its first tie, and R = 1/2 for every conclusion that is not already known directly.

== 4. the conclusion's own evidence is on BOTH sides.  i was seen three times with v; g has i and one other
      member j seen once without v. ==
   chain i -> g -> v says f = 0.50 (i's own evidence is half of the rule);  without i it says f = 0.00
   R = e from i's own evidence = 0.875.   The 'outside' evidence (i's sightings) is also inside the chain.

== 5. self-trust learned in a world with NO regularity.  Members of a category have v or not, independently,
      with probability 1/2.  Every member is seen once.  The mind then constructs  i -> g -> v  for each member
      and the senses check it later (the member's value does not change).  Hit rate of the construction: ==
   m    design: rule contains i's own evidence   leave-one-out: rule built without i   (chance = 0.500)
    2        1.000  (decided  50%)                      0.500  (decided 100%)
    3        0.750  (decided 100%)                      0.498  (decided  50%)
    5        0.688  (decided 100%)                      0.503  (decided  63%)
   10        0.664  (decided  75%)                      0.500  (decided 100%)
   30        0.584  (decided  86%)                      0.500  (decided 100%)
  100        0.543  (decided  92%)                      0.500  (decided 100%)
   -> the design's self-trust rises above chance exactly where nothing can be constructed, most for small
      categories: the construction is 'confirmed' by the member's own earlier observation, which the Up rule
      put into the rule.  Trust in 'my own construction' is identifiable only if the referee evidence is
      excluded from the construction (leave-one-out by evidence, not by tie).
